<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  App Transport Security, for PACKAGED macOS builds only.

  WHY THIS FILE EXISTS

  GRNApi fetches the live tables from http://localhost:3000. On Apple
  platforms a packaged .app refuses cleartext http:// by default — the
  request never reaches a server, the HTTP layer hands back response
  code 0, and GRNApi does exactly what it is designed to do: logs a
  warning and plays on with the tables baked into GRNTypes.h.

  That is the trap. A Mac build with ATS in the way and a Mac build with
  the server switched off and a Mac build that is working perfectly all
  look the same from inside the game. `GRN.Api.Status` in the console is
  the one-line answer; this file is the fix.

  WHEN YOU NEED IT

  - Running from the Unreal Editor (Play In Editor): you do not. The
    editor is not subject to this.
  - A packaged Mac build talking to http://: you do.
  - Talking to an https:// host: you do not — use HTTPS and delete this.

  HOW TO APPLY IT

  Unreal's packaging templates differ between engine versions, so rather
  than guess at an .ini key that may not exist in yours, merge the dict
  below into the Info.plist your build produces. In UE 5.4 the reliable
  route is Project Settings → Platforms → Mac → "Additional Plist Data",
  pasted as the inner XML; unreal/mac/connect.sh prints this path when
  it detects a packaged build.

  NOT TESTED HERE. This repository's checks run on Linux with no Unreal
  and no Mac; the contents below are the standard ATS exception and the
  reasoning is sound, but nobody has watched it work. If it does not,
  `GRN.Api.Status` will say so in one line instead of leaving you to
  guess, which was the actual point.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
	<dict>
		<!-- Local development servers only. This does NOT open the app to
		     arbitrary cleartext: everything except these two hosts still
		     goes through ATS. -->
		<key>NSExceptionDomains</key>
		<dict>
			<key>localhost</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key><true/>
				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key><true/>
			</dict>
			<key>local</key>
			<dict>
				<key>NSExceptionAllowsInsecureHTTPLoads</key><true/>
				<key>NSIncludesSubdomains</key><true/>
			</dict>
		</dict>
	</dict>
</dict>
</plist>
